7] [17592186120700 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120701 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120701 #edb/ref 1061 17520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120701 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2e04f32-eaf4-5a63-8709-0ac4376c51b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120701 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120702 #edb/ref 1061 17521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120702 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "344d2e0b-3884-5f31-a733-c6d1b647154b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120702 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120763 #edb/ref 1061 26017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a41a07f8-567e-599b-ae5d-a130ad3aae2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121043 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the vector\n\n![](images/2d/2dabd318dc49628f9333642f06492d3303617a0ea1cade93c0470c7a767121f5.svg)\n\nNow, let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be another basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = \\mathbf{c}_2 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}?$\n\nSince ![](images/3e/3e330c07260a16bc76853ca1d275bbd9759f60179dce22500ee6dd38f326ca20.svg) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal B$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-{c}_{2}+2{c}_{1} \\\\  & =-({c}_{2})-(-2{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/5d/5d39ae32e2bad5125e6d7cf8ba7b943f2c926e1f1d6525ce019fd9a40c263bce.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121043 #edb/ref 1065 3209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d5021c80-ecd5-57d1-b176-3ac06c0c4b38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121070 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/be/bec3577dd261660f2545adb66b9c26fbb520ea60aa6b0337094347dbc1937ff4.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{b}_1 = 2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2 = \\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2?$\n\nSince ![](images/20/200509c629fdf3d263ff375722f57ed77012a8a91a58a7e7f507fad3765b3782.svg) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =5(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-2({c}_{1}+{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =10{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\  & =8{c}_{1}-7{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/6b/6bc09e66faaef40bba491f31c9342d4d08a8fb6eb3fd4dd1a3e2acdd77421741.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121070 #edb/ref 1065 4287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121070 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "189050f1-7dc3-53be-a9f6-52abe95d9915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121070 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing a Vector in a Different Basis Given a Linear Relationship Between Two Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319532 #edb/ref 1080 "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319532 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319532 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c1f3a69-7740-5aa2-925c-53c1ce3286b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319705 #edb/ref 1080 "[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319705 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319705 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66b1a873-45