6 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432494 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432494 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4c066721-df4d-58dc-9748-1ef1119a9c18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432499 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f3bd7e4-e9ee-5ec1-b495-697d442c712e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432499 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2(-{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-(-{c}_{2})+3({c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & ={c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
